## P19-05 supplementary Lite request-model correction — 2026-10-25

- Run `20261025T133412Z` matched Java/Rust successful Lite subscription registration and seeded both parent and internal LMQ messages, but Lite POP on the internal `%LMQ%...%entries` name returned Broker code 29. Inspection of the unmodified 5.5.0 source shows Lite POP takes the parent topic and requires that parent topic's message type be `LITE`; Lite publishing supplies `MessageConst.PROPERTY_LITE_TOPIC` on a send to the parent, which the Broker maps to `%LMQ%$<parent>$<lite>`.
- Added a Java `send-lite` fixture path that follows this Broker contract, set the supplementary parent topic type to LITE, and added Java/Rust focused Lite POP probes that use the parent topic. The 5.3.4 baseline runner and manifest remain unchanged. `cargo fmt --all`, scoped offline `cargo check`, offline Maven compile, shell syntax, and supplementary Compose config validation pass. A new clean-source run is next.

## P19-05 supplementary Lite group binding correction — 2026-10-25

- Run `20261025T134033Z` reached Lite POP on the LITE parent after Java and Rust each matched successful subscription registration, but Broker response code 29 reported `subscription bind topic not match` for the Lite group. Apache 5.5.0 `PopLiteMessageProcessor` requires `SubscriptionGroupConfig.liteBindTopic` to equal the request parent topic; the first supplementary setup created ordinary groups without that attribute.
- Updated the disposable `updateSubGroup` commands to set `+lite.bind.topic=<parent>` on each language's Lite group. The supplementary message now seeds before Java/Rust subscription registration so registration sees the active derived Lite queue. The runner again preserves the 5.3.4 row and cleans only its isolated resources.

## P19-05 supplementary LMQ store configuration — 2026-10-25

- Run `20261025T134349Z` passed the group bind check, and both languages again matched Lite subscription registration. Lite POP returned `POLLING_NOT_FOUND` because the stored Lite message had no active LMQ dispatch queue. Apache 5.5.0 `MessageStoreConfig.enableLmq` defaults to false, so the supplementary Broker was not writing Lite LMQ consume queues.
- Added a separate `broker-lite-5.5.0.conf` with `enableLmq=true` and mounted it only in the supplementary Compose profile. The mandatory 5.3.4 Broker configuration remains unchanged. Shell syntax, Compose configuration, and whitespace checks pass before the next run.

